then kick off.  I would have to push the starter switch once to
reset the PSU, then the second time it would turn the fans
another rev.  I guess the board is drawing too much current for
the PSU.  But I don't know  ... has anyone run a UP2000 with a
consumer PSU?  Is my PSU kicking out because it can't handle the
draw, or is it because the4 board is shorted out?

The UP2000 manual says you need 70 amps from one of the outputs,
50 from another (i forget which) whihc is way more than anyu
consumer PSU can supply. Maurice said you need the full 750W to
run the UP2000 with 2 processors, and the unit costs $750.

I need to know if the mobo and cpus are good.  If they are I'll
keep them and try to find a power supply.  If not, I should get
my money back.  Can anyone think of a way to test them that
doesn't involve buying a $750 power supply?

I just won a 400W astec PSU on eBay.  I may get lucky and get
the one with 50 amps on the 5V output.  Maybe I can use that to
test this setup.  Anyway I have about 20 days to figure it out.

Off-list reply is OK, if there is interest I can post a summary,
like on the Tru64 list.  Thanks for your help.

