On Tuesday 09 September 2003 10:05 am, Jon Norstog wrote:
> I should have read the fine print.  The seller said it had a
> bad power supply and wasn't guaranteed.  I did pay for the
> paypal satisfaction guarantee, which I may exercise.  But
> first I want to see if the mobo and CPUs are good.
>
> Maurice Hilarius helped me a little with some info and
> suggestions.  I tested the power supply with no load, all the
> outputs were to spec but hooked to a late-model Athlon PC it
> only ran for about 10 seconds.

Hmmm...definitely sounds like a bad PSU.  FWIW, I recall those 
units having removable fuses.  You might try replacing the fuse 
if it's cheap.  It's a long shot though--in my experience, fuses 
are usually either 100% working or 100% dead.

> The UP2000 manual says you need 70 amps from one of the
> outputs, 50 from another (i forget which) whihc is way more
> than anyu consumer PSU can supply. Maurice said you need the
> full 750W to run the UP2000 with 2 processors, and the unit
> costs $750.

Well, there are other sources of 750W power subsystems than that 
thing.  Maybe not cheaper though.  I actually run two ATX 
systems with that kind of power capacity, but they weren't cheap 
and they weren't all that easy to set up.  The cheapest setup I 
know of with close to that kind of capacity would come from PC 
Power & Cooling's redundant PSU line, and the one I bought was 
an absolute pain in the anorak to install.  My main chassis 
could probably do it (SuperMicro SC850), but that beast cost 
over $1000 when I got it.

> I need to know if the mobo and cpus are good.  If they are
> I'll keep them and try to find a power supply.  If not, I
> should get my money back.  Can anyone think of a way to test
> them that doesn't involve buying a $750 power supply?

Well, on the off-chance you haven't tried this, I'd suggest 
trying it with just a single CPU, minimal memory configuration, 
and no card/drive load.  Swap out parts until you've tested 
every DIMM and both CPUs.  If that works, then...well...you'll 
be pretty sure it's good.  If not, you're not any worse off 
except for lost time.

(My recollection is that you'll need a minimum of two DIMMs per 
installed CPU on that thing, but I don't know how the DIMM banks 
are laid out.)

I hope you actually get that thing working...it would be one 
really sweet Alpha rig!

-- 
